Yes — with the RetryConfig, the situation is clearer. You do not need to change RetryConfig for the six errors you listed, assuming they are returned as HTTP 400 responses, which your logs indicate.

Why?

Your retry condition is:

public boolean isRetryable(Throwable throwable) {
    if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
        int statusCode = ex.getStatusCode().value();

        return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;
    }

    return true;
}

So:

Error	HTTP status	Retried?	Final logging

SYSTEM.UNEXPECTED.ERROR	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
No valid address suggestions	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
STANDARDIZED.ADDRESS.NOTFOUND	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
INTERPOLATED.STREET.ADDRESS	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
STATEORPROVINCECODE.TOO.LONG	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
INVALID.SUITE.NUMBER	400	❌ No	Should be WARN
Other 400	400	❌ No	Existing ERROR behavior
500+	500–599	✅ Yes	ERROR if retries exhausted


So the retry configuration already does the right thing for the validation errors.


